 line even after error, but error_line is fixed at first error. The trajectory should contain all steps regardless of validity? The specification doesn't say to stop at error. Since trajectory is just the steps, we can collect all. We'll keep building.\n        trajectory.append({\"type\": type_, \"content\": content})\n        # check state transition\n        if type_ == \"Thought\":\n            if state in ['start', 'after_observation']:\n                state = 'after_thought'\n            else:\n                if valid: # first error\n                    valid = False\n                    error_line = i\n        elif type_ == \"Action\":\n            if state == 'after_thought':\n                state = 'after_action'\n            else:\n                if valid:\n                    valid = False\n                    error_line = i\n        elif type_ == \"Observation\":\n            if state == 'after_action':\n                state = 'after_observation'\n            else:\n                if valid:\n                    valid = False\n                    error_line = i\n        elif type_ == \"Final\":\n            if state == 'after_thought':\n                state = 'end'\n            else:\n                if valid:\n                    valid = False\n                    error_line = i\n        else:\n            # unknown type, treat as invalid\n            if valid:\n                valid = False\n                error_line = i\n            # keep state unchanged? Probably mark invalid.\n    # after loop, if still valid and state != 'end', it means no Final at end -> invalid\n    if valid and state != 'end':\n        valid = False\n        error_line = len(lines) - 1  # the last line is not Final; but we've already set error_line? Not yet.\n        # Could also set to len(lines)-1 because the last step is the violation.\n    return {\"valid\": valid, \"error_line\": error_line, \"trajectory\": trajectory}\n\nBut careful: if after loop valid and state =