ut implementation as JSON string under key \"implementation\". The entire response must be a JSON object with single key \"implementation\" whose value is a string of Python source code. The source must define solve(case). We must escape newlines, backslashes, quotes.\n\nWrite the Python code as a string. Use triple quotes? Actually we need to escape inside JSON string. So we'll write code as a normal string with \\n for newlines.\n\nWe'll produce:\n\n{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    lines = case['lines']\\n    trajectory = []\\n    valid = True\\n    error_line = None\\n    state = 'start'\\n    for i, line in enumerate(lines):\\n        if ': ' in line:\\n            typ, content = line.split(': ', 1)\\n        else:\\n            typ = 'Invalid'\\n            content = line\\n        trajectory.append({'type': typ, 'content': content})\\n        if valid:\\n            if typ == 'Thought':\\n                if state in ['start', 'after_observation']:\\n                    state = 'after_thought'\\n                else:\\n                    valid = False\\n                    error_line = i\\n            elif typ == 'Action':\\n                if state == 'after_thought':\\n                    state = 'after_action'\\n                else:\\n                    valid = False\\n                    error_line = i\\n            elif typ == 'Observation':\\n                if state == 'after_action':\\n                    state = 'after_observation'\\n                else:\\n                    valid = False\\n                    error_line = i\\n            elif typ == 'Final':\\n                if state == 'after_thought':\\n                    state = 'end'\\n                else:\\n                    valid = False\\n                    error_line = i\\n            else:\\n                valid = False\\n                error_line = i\\n    if valid and state != 'end':\\n        valid = False\\n        error_line = len(lines) - 1\\n    return {'valid': valid, 'error_line': error_line, 'trajectory': trajectory}\"\n}\n\nBut need to ensure that the string is properly escaped. In JSON, we need to escape backslashes and double quotes. So we'll replace each \\ with \\\\ and each \" with \\\" and newline with \\n. The above string contains double quotes, so we must escape them. Also backslashes? The code has only one backslash? Actually no backslash except maybe in line split? The code uses ': ', no backslash. So only quotes.\n\nAlternatively, we can write the code using single quotes for strings to avoid escaping double quotes in JSON. But the code uses double quotes for dict keys and strings. We can use single quotes in the Python code to simplify JSON escaping: e.g., use 'lines' instead of \"lines\". But the content strings like 'Thought' etc. can be single quotes. That would reduce need to escape double quotes. However, JSON string value can contain single quotes without escaping. So we can write the Python code with single quotes. That's easier.\n\nLet's rewrite with single quotes where possible:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    lines = case['lines']\n    trajectory = []\n    valid = True\n    error_line = None\n    state = 'start'\n    for i, line in enumerate(lines):\n        if ': ' in line:\n            typ, content = line.split(': ', 1)\n        else:\n            typ = 'Invalid'\n            content = line\n        trajectory.append({'type': typ, 'content': content})\n        if valid:\n            if typ == 'Thought':\n                if state in ['start', 'after_observation']:\n                    state = 'after_thought'\n                else:\n                    valid = False\n                    error_line = i\n            elif typ == 'Action':\n                if state == 'after_thought':\n                    state = 'after_action'\n                else:\n                    valid = False\n                    error_line = i\n            elif typ == 'Observation':\n                if state == 'after_action